From 22ddca24e11acf57900122e0d7212cecdbb0e588 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pdale-boop <237306950+pdale-boop@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 20:15:19 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] Aurora: the HD look's shading leaves the effects drawn over
 the scene alone

The sun shadows, the occlusion and the cloud shadows are worked out after the
scene from its depth buffer. An effect drawn blended without writing depth (the
spin attack's rings and burst, the Master Sword's glow, sparks) leaves the
depth there the scenery's behind it, so it was shaded as that scenery: Link's
cast shadow was drawn across the glowing rings over the grass. Wind Waker HD
draws such effects after its lighting, and they aren't shadowed.

The scene pass's effects are drawn again, with their own pipelines, into a mask
of their own (post_fx::effect_mask_target(), in the frame buffer's format, at
the scene's size), over black and against the pass's finished depth, so each
pixel holds what the effects added there. The sun shadows (the shadow map's and
the screen's), the occlusion and the cloud shadows take away as much of their
shade as the effects make up: their brightest channel times 2
(DOL_AURORA_EFFECT_MASK_GAIN), so a glow over the ground is left as it is and a
faint one is mostly shaded with what is behind it. Only within 3,000 units,
fading out by 6,000: far off, what is drawn so is the haze over the sea toward
the horizon, which the cloud shadows shade with the sea.

An effect here is a 3D draw that is blended (not subtracted), writes colour,
tests depth and writes none (gxcore_draw.cpp effect_draw()). Left out:
- the sea's own blended draws (the surf, the ring of sea round an island);
- the GameCube's own shadows, blended by the frame buffer's alpha;
- what has no depth test (the sky's clouds): its pixels are those the draws
  after it leave, which a mask drawn against the finished depth can't tell, and
  the sky's clouds lit up shadows on the land drawn over them;
- a skinned model's blended draws other than added light: a character's eyes
  and mouth are on its face and take its shade; the Master Sword's glow is
  added light and is kept.
The effects are drawn into the mask 5% of their distance further back
(DOL_AURORA_EFFECT_MASK_PUSH, as a smaller depth range), so what lies on a
surface (a grass fringe along a cliff's edge, a decal) fails the depth test
and keeps the surface's shade.

The mask is drawn only while the sun shadows, the occlusion or the cloud
shadows are on, single-sampled and at the frame buffer's size, and only for a
pass with effects; a frame without any binds a black texel.
DOL_AURORA_EFFECT_MASK_DEBUG=1 shows in red what the sun shadows leave.

Effects that write depth are effects too. Two kinds in Dragon Roost Cavern
weren't, so the sun pass shaded them as scenery:

- The lava geysers: blended, alpha-tested models that write depth, their
  colour from the TEV's registers alone (no texture or rasterized colour).
  In the room's shade they went blue (the shade's ambient over the sunlight).
- The dust a shutting door raises: each particle drawn alpha-tested into the
  depth alone, then in colour over it. The floor under it was shaded and
  fogged at the dust's depth, so the dust showed as dark, sharp-edged holes
  in the room's haze.

Now a blended draw that writes depth is an effect when it is one of the
game's particles (the host's tag) or a glow model (its colour from neither a
texture nor the rasterized colour). A particle's colour draw over its own
depth draw is drawn at its own depth into the effects' mask, as is an effect
that writes depth. Such a pass's effects' mask tests against a copy of the
sea mask's depth, which leaves those effects out: the others first, pushed
back as before, then those at their own depth (a geyser's sparks on its
surface, pushed back against the geyser's depth, kept its shade).

Where such an effect covers a pixel, the occlusion, the sun shadows and the
cloud shadows shade from the sea mask's depth (the scenery under it, which
Wind Waker HD lights before it draws its effects): a small pass makes that
depth only in frames with such effects. Other frames are unchanged (frame
dumps from five save states match the build before within noise).

Two Dragon Roost Cavern effects from Dale's play (Nov 3):

- The lava geysers' pale core stayed blue in the shade: of each geyser's
  three large layers, the middle one adds half the game's light to its
  colour (C1 + 0.5 x RASC), so it wasn't a glow model and was shaded as
  scenery. A glow model may now take the rasterized colour when its
  textures only cut its shape (their alpha alone is used); untextured,
  that colour is lit scenery's. And an effect drawn at its own depth (a
  geyser in front of the far wall it hides) keeps its share near or far:
  the cover faded out from 3,000 units by the depth behind it, the far
  wall's, and the geysers, about 3,000 away, took part of the shade.
- The dust a shutting door raises was too see-through: a dark effect
  blended over the scene adds little over black, so its share (its
  brightest channel times 2) left most of the pixel shaded as the floor
  behind it, and the floor's shadows showed through. The effects are now
  drawn into the mask a second time over white: 1 - white + black is a
  blended effect's opacity (an added one's colour, as before), and the
  larger of the two is its share. DOL_AURORA_EFFECT_MASK_OPACITY=0 keeps
  the colour alone.

A small pass makes the cover the shading reads from both masks, with the
own-depth flag in its alpha. With the look off, nothing changes; the
look's frames without effects are unchanged, and frame dumps from four
other save states match within noise.
